.Dd September 1, 2026
.Dt ZKCMP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zkcmp
.Nd zero-knowledge file comparison utility
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm commit
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl H Ar hash
.Ar file
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm prove
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl H Ar hash
.Ar file
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm verify
.Op Fl hs
.Op Fl H Ar hash
.Ar commit
.Ar proof
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm check
.Op Fl hs
.Op Fl H Ar hash
.Ar file
.Ar proof
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility provides zero-knowledge comparison and proof operations for files.
It allows a prover to demonstrate that a file matches a commitment without
revealing the file's cryptographic hash.

The
.Nm
utility provides four subcommands:
.Cm commit ,
.Cm prove ,
.Cm verify ,
and
.Cm check .
The
.Cm commit
and
.Cm prove
commands operate on a file and produce a commitment or proof on standard
output.
The
.Cm verify
command verifies a proof against a public commitment.
The
.Cm check
command verifies a proof against the corresponding file.

.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are available to
.Cm commit
and
.Cm prove :

.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h
Do not follow symbolic links.

.It Fl H Ar hash
Select the hash algorithm.
Supported values are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar sha256
SHA-256. This is the default.
.It Ar sha3-256
SHA3-256.
.It Ar blake2s-256
BLAKE2s-256.
.El

The same hash algorithm must be selected when generating and checking
cryptographic values.
.El

The following additional option is available to
.Cm verify
and
.Cm check :

.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl s
Print nothing when verification fails; return exit status only.
.El

.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "Cm verify"
.It Cm commit Ar file
Hash
.Ar file
and generate a Base64-encoded public commitment.

.It Cm prove Ar file
Generate a Base64-encoded zero-knowledge proof for
.Ar file .

.It Cm verify Ar commit Ar proof
Verify a zero-knowledge proof against a public commitment.

The
.Ar commit
and
.Ar proof
arguments may be provided as literal parameters, file paths, or
.Cm -
to read the parameter from standard input.

.It Cm check Ar file Ar proof
Hash
.Ar file
and verify the proof supplied in
.Ar proof .

The
.Ar proof
argument may be provided as a literal parameter, a file path, or
.Cm -
to read the proof from standard input.
.El

.Sh PROTOCOL
The zero-knowledge protocol is based on a Schnorr-style proof using the
3072-bit Modular Exponentiation Group defined by RFC 3526.

The file is hashed using the selected hash algorithm.
The resulting digest is used to derive the secret value for the proof.
The proof uses a Fiat-Shamir challenge to provide a non-interactive
verification protocol.

.Sh EXAMPLES
Generate a commitment for a file:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp commit file > commit
.Ed

Generate a proof for a file:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp prove file > proof
.Ed

Verify a commitment and proof:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp verify commit proof
.Ed

Generate and verify a proof through a Unix pipeline:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp prove file | zkcmp check file -
.Ed

Generate a proof locally and verify it on a remote system:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp prove file | ssh host 'zkcmp check file -'
.Ed

Generate a commitment and proof once and allow multiple parties to verify
them independently:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp commit file > commit
$ zkcmp prove file > proof
$ zkcmp verify commit proof
$ ssh host1 'zkcmp verify commit proof'
$ ssh host2 'zkcmp verify commit proof'
.Ed

Use SHA3-256 when generating a proof:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp prove -H sha3-256 file
.Ed

.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The operation completed or the proof verified successfully.

.It 1
The proof did not match.

.It 2
An operational error occurred.
.El

.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cmp 1 ,
.Xr diff 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Nami Arjmandi
.El
